## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to design algorithms with provable performance guarantees, handle pathological inputs gracefully, and understand trade-offs between different sorting approaches. It's particularly relevant for large-scale systems where worst-case behavior impacts reliability.

## Key Concepts
- **Introsort**: Hybrid algorithm combining quicksort's average-case efficiency with heapsort's worst-case guarantees
- **3-way partitioning**: Dutch national flag algorithm that efficiently handles duplicate keys
- **Depth monitoring**: Switches to heapsort when recursion depth exceeds 2⌊log₂(n)⌋ to maintain O(n log n) worst-case
- **Space complexity**: O(log n) stack space through tail-call optimization

## Code Example
```javascript
function introsort(arr) {
  const n = arr.length;
  const maxDepth = Math.floor(2 * Math.log2(n || 1));

  function sort(left, right, depth) {
    if (left >= right) return;

    if (depth === 0) {
      heapSort(arr, left, right);
      return;
    }

    const [lt, gt] = threeWayPartition(arr, left, right);

    // Sort smaller partition first (tail recursion optimization)
    if (lt - left < right - gt) {
      sort(left, lt - 1, depth - 1);
      sort(gt + 1, right, depth - 1);
    } else {
      sort(gt + 1, right, depth - 1);
      sort(left, lt - 1, depth - 1);
    }
  }

  sort(0, n - 1, maxDepth);
  return arr;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests handling of multiple shortest paths and second-best paths with counting, using a single Dijkstra variant. It blends graph traversal and path combinatorics under constraints typical at scale.

## Key Concepts
- Two-best paths per node; counting paths for each distance tier
- Dijkstra relaxation with tie-handling
- Modulo counting for combinatorial paths

## Code Example
```javascript
// Two-best Dijkstra (conceptual)
function secondShortest(graph, s, t, MOD){
  const INF = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY;
  const n = graph.length;
  const dist1 = Array(n).fill(INF);
  const dist2 = Array(n).fill(INF);
  const cnt1 = Array(n).fill(0);
  const cnt2 = Array(n).fill(0);
  dist1[s] = 0; cnt1[s] = 1;
  const pq = new MinHeap();
  pq.push([0, s]);
  while(!pq.isEmpty()){
    const [d, u] = pq.pop();
    if (d > dist2[u]) continue;
    for (const [v, w] of graph[u]){
      const nd = d + w;
      if (nd < dist1[v]){
        dist2[v] = dist1[v]; cnt2[v] = cnt1[v];
        dist1[v] = nd; cnt1[v] = cnt1[u];
        pq.push([nd, v]);
      } else if (nd === dist1[v]){
        cnt1[v] = (cnt1[v] + cnt1[u]) % MOD;
      } else if (nd > dist1[v] && nd < dist2[v]){
        dist2[v] = nd; cnt2[v] = cnt1[u];
        pq.push([nd, v]);
      } else if (nd === dist2[v]){
        cnt2[v] = (cnt2[v] + cnt1[u]) % MOD;
      }
    }
  }
  return [dist2[t], cnt2[t] % MOD];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to count distinct second-shortest paths that share subpaths?  
- How does this adapt if negative weights are allowed but no negative cycles exist?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests understanding of in-place sorting algorithms, particularly the challenging problem of merging sorted subarrays without auxiliary space. It distinguishes candidates who understand the theoretical difference between standard merge sort (O(n) space) and true in-place variants, and reveals familiarity with classical algorithms like the Ford-Johnson merge sort or rotation-based merging.

## Key Concepts

- **In-place merge**: Merging two sorted subarrays within the original array without allocating a separate buffer
- **Stability**: Preserving the relative order of equal elements, critical for multi-key sorting
- **Bottom-up merge sort**: Iterative approach that starts with singleton runs and doubles the merge width each pass
- **Rotation-based merging**: Using cyclic block swaps to interleave elements from two sorted segments
- **Time/space complexities**: O(n log n) average time, O(n²) worst-case due to rotation cost; O(1) extra space

## Code Example

```javascript
function rotate(arr, l, m, r) {
  // Rotates arr[l..m-1] with arr[m..r-1] in-place
  // Uses block-swap: hold arr[l], shift left block right by one, repeat cyclically
}

function inPlaceMerge(arr, l, m, r) {
  // Merge sorted runs [l, m) and [m, r) in-place
  // Find the smaller run, rotate it into position
}

function inPlaceMergeSort(arr) {
  // Bottom-up: start with width=1, double each iteration
  for (let w = 1; w < arr.length; w *= 2) {
    for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i += 2 * w) {
      inPlaceMerge(arr, i, Math.min(i + w, arr.length), Math.min(i + 2 * w, arr.length));
    }
  }
}
```

**In-place example**: arr = [1, 3, 5 | 2, 4, 6], merging runs [1,3,5] and [2,4,6] rotates to [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6].

## Follow-up Questions

- How does rotation cost O(n) per merge affect constants and worst-case performance?
- How would you adapt this for linked data structures?
- What optimizations would improve practical performance despite theoretical worst-case?
- How does this compare to in-place quicksort or heap sort?

## Why This Is Asked
Autocomplete for large vocabularies is common; a Trie yields fast prefix queries and ordered counts.

## Key Concepts
- Trie vs BST for prefix-based lookup
- DFS with pruning and max-heap to collect top-k results
- Memory optimizations: sparse maps, compact nodes

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Map();
    this.word = null;
    this.freq = 0;
  }
}

class Trie {
  constructor() {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
  }

  insert(word, delta) {
    let cur = this.root;
    for (const ch of word) {
      if (!cur.next.has(ch)) {
        cur.next.set(ch, new TrieNode());
      }
      cur = cur.next.get(ch);
    }
    cur.word = word;
    cur.freq += delta;
  }

  search(word) {
    let cur = this.root;
    for (const ch of word) {
      if (!cur.next.has(ch)) return false;
      cur = cur.next.get(ch);
    }
    return cur.word === word;
  }

  prefixTop(prefix, k) {
    let cur = this.root;
    for (const ch of prefix) {
      if (!cur.next.has(ch)) return [];
      cur = cur.next.get(ch);
    }

    const results = [];
    const dfs = (node) => {
      if (node.word) {
        results.push({ word: node.word, freq: node.freq });
        if (results.length > k) {
          results.sort((a, b) => b.freq - a.freq);
          results.pop();
        }
      }
      for (const child of node.next.values()) {
        dfs(child);
      }
    };

    dfs(cur);
    return results.sort((a, b) => b.freq - a.freq).slice(0, k).map(r => r.word);
  }
}
```

## Time/Space Trade-offs vs BST/AVL
- **Trie**: O(m) insert/search where m = word length, independent of vocabulary size. Prefix queries O(m + k) where k = results. Space O(α×m) where α = alphabet size.
- **BST/AVL**: O(log n) operations where n = vocabulary size. Prefix queries require O(n) traversal. Space O(n).
- **Trie advantage**: Constant-time prefix lookups, natural ordering by frequency, better cache locality for common prefixes.
- **BST advantage**: More memory efficient for sparse vocabularies, simpler implementation.

## Memory-heavy Vocabulary Justification
For large vocabularies, use:
- **Sparse maps** (Map/Object) instead of fixed arrays for children
- **Node compression** (radix/patricia tries) to merge common prefixes
- **Frequency-based pruning** to remove low-frequency words
- **External storage** for very large datasets, keeping hot prefixes in memory

Tries excel when prefix queries dominate and memory can be optimized through compression techniques.

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to model DP with obstacles and variable step sizes, requiring careful base cases and transition logic. It also assesses both memoization and tabulation familiarity.

## Key Concepts

- DP with multiple transitions (1..k)
- Handling blocked steps
- Memoization vs tabulation
- Time complexity: O(n*maxJump); Space: O(n) (or O(maxJump) with optimization)

## Code Example

```javascript
// Bottom-up tabulation
function countWays(n, maxJump, blocked) {
  const dp = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 1;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    if (blocked[i]) { dp[i] = 0; continue; }
    for (let j = 1; j <= maxJump; j++) {
      if (i - j >= 0) dp[i] += dp[i - j];
    }
  }
  return dp[n];
}
```

```javascript
// Memoized recursive version
function countWaysRec(n, maxJump, blocked, i = n, memo = {}) {
  if (i === 0) return 1;
  if (i < 0 || blocked[i]) return 0;
  if (memo[i] !== undefined) return memo[i];
  let ways = 0;
  for (let t = 1; t <= maxJump; t++) ways += countWaysRec(n, maxJump, blocked, i - t, memo);
  memo[i] = ways;
  return ways;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize to O(maxJump) space with a sliding window?
- How would you adjust for modulo arithmetic to prevent overflow for large n?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests fundamental graph traversal algorithms, BFS implementation, and pathfinding skills - critical for autonomous vehicle navigation systems and route planning applications.

## Expected Answer
The candidate should implement BFS using a queue, maintain a visited set to avoid cycles, track distances from the start node, and handle edge cases such as when start equals target, disconnected graphs, or empty input. They should also explain why BFS guarantees the shortest path in unweighted graphs.

## Code Example
```python
from collections import deque

def shortest_path(graph, start, end):
    if start == end:
        return 0

    queue = deque([(start, 0)])
    visited = {start}

    while queue:
        node, distance = queue.popleft()

        for neighbor in graph.get(node, []):
            if neighbor == end:
                return distance + 1
            if neighbor not in visited:
                visited.add(neighbor)
                queue.append((neighbor, distance + 1))

    return -1
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes understanding of introsort-style hybrids, balancing average performance with worst-case guarantees in production environments. It tests pivot strategies, partitioning, depth tracking, and in-place constraints.

## Key Concepts

- Introsort: hybrid of quicksort and heapsort with depth-based switch
- Median-of-three pivot: reduces chance of worst-case partitions
- 3-way partitioning: handles duplicates efficiently
- Insertion sort for tiny subarrays: amortized gains

## Code Example

```javascript
function introsort(arr) {
  const maxDepth = Math.floor(Math.log2(arr.length)) * 2;
  function insertionSort(a, lo, hi) {
    for (let i = lo + 1; i < hi; i++) {
      let key = a[i], j = i - 1;
      while (j >= lo && a[j] > key) { a[j + 1] = a[j]; j--; }
      a[j + 1] = key;
    }
  }
  function heapify(a, count, i, offset) {
    // placeholder for in-place heapify within [offset, offset+count)
  }
  function partition(a, lo, hi) {
    // median-of-three and 3-way partitioning placeholder
    return Math.floor((lo + hi) / 2);
  }
  function sort(a, lo, hi, depth) {
    if (hi - lo <= 16) { insertionSort(a, lo, hi); return; }
    if (depth > maxDepth) { /* heapsort(a, lo, hi); */ return; }
    const pivot = partition(a, lo, hi);
    // recursively sort partitions
    sort(a, lo, pivot, depth + 1);
    sort(a, pivot, hi, depth + 1);
  }
  sort(arr, 0, arr.length, 0);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this to be stable, or explain why stability is costly in-place?
- Compare this approach with TimSort or other stable hybrids in real-world libraries.
- What are the practical pitfalls when implementing pivot selection and depth bounds in a multi-threaded setting?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to model task dependencies as a DAG and compute an optimal parallel schedule using topological ordering and dynamic programming.

## Key Concepts

- **Topological sort**: Ordering nodes such that all prerequisites come before dependent tasks
- **DAG DP**: Computing the longest path in a directed acyclic graph
- **Cycle detection**: Using indegree counts to identify cycles (if not all nodes are processed)
- **Time complexity**: O(n + m) where n is nodes and m is edges

## Code Example

```javascript
function earliestFinish(n, edges, duration) {
  const adj = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);
  const indeg = Array(n).fill(0);

  for (const [u, v] of edges) {
    adj[u].push(v);
    indeg[v]++;
  }

  const finish = duration.slice();
  const q = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if (indeg[i] === 0) q.push(i);
  }

  let processed = 0;

  while (q.length) {
    const u = q.shift();
    processed++;

    for (const v of adj[u]) {
      finish[v] = Math.max(finish[v], finish[u] + duration[v]);
      indeg[v]--;
      if (indeg[v] === 0) q.push(v);
    }
  }

  if (processed !== n) return -1;
  return Math.max(...finish);
}
```

## Key Takeaways

- Unlimited workers means tasks run in parallel when dependencies allow
- The finish time of a task is the maximum of (prerequisite finish + its own duration)
- The pipeline completes when the slowest path (critical path) finishes

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical DP reasoning: variable step costs require deriving both a memoized and a tabular solution, handle unreachable states, and discuss trade-offs.

## Key Concepts

- Top-down DP with memoization
- Bottom-up DP (tabulation)
- Handling unreachable states with Infinity
- Time/space complexity: O(n|S|) time, O(n) space

## Code Example

```javascript
function minCostTopDown(n, steps, memo=new Map()){
  if(n===0) return 0
  if(n<0) return Infinity
  if(memo.has(n)) return memo.get(n)
  let best = Infinity
  for(let s of steps){
    const c = s + minCostTopDown(n-s, steps, memo)
    if(c < best) best = c
  }
  memo.set(n, best)
  return best
}

function minCostBottomUp(n, steps){
  const INF = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY
  const dp = Array(n+1).fill(INF)
  dp[0] = 0
  for(let i=1; i<=n; i++){
    for(let s of steps){
      if(i>=s && dp[i-s] !== INF){
        dp[i] = Math.min(dp[i], s + dp[i-s])
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[n] === INF ? -1 : dp[n]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt if costs were a non-linear function cost(s) instead of s? 
- How would you extend to count the number of ways to reach n with min cost, not just the cost itself?

## Purpose
This problem tests sliding-window aggregation with per-key state management, lazy expiration strategies, and top-K extraction using a heap. It integrates multiple data structures: hashtables for O(1) key lookups, per-key queues for temporal ordering, and a heap for maintaining ranked sums.

## Key Concepts
- **Sliding window maintenance**: Events expire once they exceed the window width W
- **Per-key accumulation**: Each key maintains its own queue of (timestamp, value) pairs
- **Lazy eviction**: Stale entries are removed only when encountered, avoiding unnecessary cleanup
- **Amortized efficiency**: The cost of expiration is distributed across active operations

## Code Example
```javascript
class WindowedTopKMap {
  constructor(windowWidth) {
    this.W = windowWidth;
    this.map = new Map();
    this.heap = new MinHeap();
  }

  addEvent(key, value, ts) {
    if (!this.map.has(key)) {
      this.map.set(key, { sum: 0, queue: [] });
    }
    const entry = this.map.get(key);
    entry.queue.push({ ts, value });
    entry.sum += value;
    this.heap.push({ sum: entry.sum, key });
  }

  advanceTime(now) {
    // Lazy cleanup handled in topKKeys
  }

  topKKeys(K, now) {
    const results = [];
    while (results.length < K && !this.heap.isEmpty()) {
      const top = this.heap.pop();
      const entry = this.map.get(top.key);
      if (!entry) continue;
      this._expire(entry.queue, now);
      if (Math.abs(entry.sum - top.sum) > 0.0001) continue;
      results.push(top.key);
    }
    return results;
  }

  _expire(queue, now) {
    while (queue.length && queue[0].ts <= now - this.W) {
      entry.sum -= queue.shift().value;
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle negative values, where sum could decrease over time?
- Could you support variable window sizes per key if needed?
- What memory bounds exist, and how could you proactively prune empty keys?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes understanding of sorting trade-offs (in-place memory vs stability vs constants) and how hybrid approaches balance speed, especially relevant in finance and large-scale data.

## Key Concepts

- 3-way partitioning in quicksort
- Insertion sort for small slices
- In-place sorting and O(1) extra space
- Average vs worst-case complexities; pivot choice

## Code Example

```javascript
function sortHybrid(a){
  const TH = 16;
  const swap=(i,j)=>{ [a[i],a[j]]=[a[j],a[i]]; };
  const insertSort=(l,r)=>{
    for(let i=l+1;i<=r;i++){
      let x=a[i], j=i-1;
      while(j>=l && a[j]>x){ a[j+1]=a[j]; j--; }
      a[j+1]=x;
    }
  };
  const partition=(lo,hi)=>{
    const p=a[lo+((hi-lo)>>1)];
    let i=lo, lt=lo, gt=hi;
    while(i<=gt){
      if(a[i]<p){ swap(i,lt); i++; lt++; }
      else if(a[i]>p){ swap(i,gt); gt--; }
      else i++;
    }
    return [lt,gt];
  };
  const qs=(lo,hi)=>{
    if(lo>=hi) return;
    if(hi-lo+1 <= TH){ insertSort(lo,hi); return; }
    const [l,r] = partition(lo,hi);
    qs(lo, l-1);
    qs(r+1, hi);
  };
  qs(0,a.length-1);
}
```
